From: wart maintainer
Subject: [PATCH] DRC: NEGATIVE-RESULT — replace FreeList::first_fit O(F) with O(log F)

**This patch was tested and made the wart-app workload WORSE.** Posted
here only as a benchmark fixture so reviewers can see why allocator-
side speedups alone don't help, and as a starting point for anyone
exploring this corner of the design space.

The change: add a parallel `BTreeSet<(len, index)>` to `FreeList`,
maintained in lockstep with the existing `BTreeMap<index, len>` via
three helpers. Replace `first_fit`'s `iter().find(...)` linear scan
with `range((alloc_size, 0)..).next()` — O(log F) instead of O(F).
Policy changes from first-fit-by-address to best-fit-by-size, ties
broken by lowest address.

All 12 existing FreeList unit tests pass.

On device (Pixel 2 XL, wart-app + Compose Material3, 2026-05-18):

| Metric        | Unpatched | With this patch | Δ      |
|---------------|-----------|-----------------|--------|
| Sweep 1 dur   | 472 ms    | 823 ms          | +74%   |
| Sweep 2 dur   | 1222 ms   | 2010 ms         | +64%   |
| Sweep 3 dur   | (n/a)     | 4314 ms         | new    |
| Frame mean    | ~16 ms    | ~16 ms          | none   |
| Guest alloc rate | 15K refs/s | 19K refs/s   | +27%   |

Two reasons it made things worse for our workload:

1. `first_fit` was not the bottleneck. Frame-mean cost is dominated by
   Compose's render/layout/draw work; alloc-path microseconds we shaved
   off didn't appear in any frame-level metric.

2. Faster allocs made the leak worse. With cheaper allocs, the
   Kotlin/Wasm guest churns SafeContinuation cycles faster, so N grew
   faster between sweeps. Each subsequent sweep has more to walk + more
   to dec_ref. The per-dealloc overhead from maintaining a second data
   structure (one extra BTreeSet op per add_block / remove_block /
   update_block_len) then compounds over N+ deallocations per sweep.

The conclusion is that any allocator-side optimization for this
workload class must be paired with sweep-side improvements (e.g.
auto-scheduled gc, contiguous-storage over-approx set, incremental
sweep). On its own, it accelerates the pathology.

Applies on top of 01-instrumentation.patch.

---

--- a/src/runtime/vm/gc/enabled/free_list.rs
+++ b/src/runtime/vm/gc/enabled/free_list.rs
@@ -1,5 +1,5 @@
